  "method": "road graph with a node at every vertex; refuge = a node incident to a segment surviving the 50 ft major stage; isolation = widest-path bottleneck to that set, computed by one descending union-find pass and cross-checked by BFS at every sampled stage. Segment isolation is capped by the segment's own flood stage.",
  "method_segment_aggregation": "max over the segment's vertices (last exit to go), capped by the segment's own flood_stage_ft",
  "caveats": "Reachability is a road-network property, not a safety guarantee: a refuge node is ground the modelled water does not reach at 50 ft, not a shelter, and not validated against where people actually went. Absolute values inherit the -0.92 ft/mile HAND-as-stage error documented in road_thresholds.py, so they move when that primitive is fixed; the structure of who is trapped first is what this stage adds. The corridor is a bounding-box clip, so roads are cut at its edge: escape to high ground outside the study area is NOT counted as an exit, which under-reports reachability for fragments touching the boundary. Treating the box edge as an exit instead moves no_dry_refuge from 883 to 674 and trapped_before_flood from 230 to 408; the numbers here use the stricter reading."
